 "text": " Now it is your turn to think like a hardware engineer. Imagine dusk is falling and a passing cloud causes our light sensor reading to flicker 450 then 280 then 305. If we use a simple threshold that turns the lamp on whenever light is below 300 and off whenever it is 300 or above, what happens to the lamp across the three readings and how does adding a buffer gap called hysteresis turning on below 290 and turning off only above 320. Fix the problem. Pause the video for 10 seconds and predict the lamp states for both rules. Ready? Under a simple threshold of 300, the lamp goes off, then on, then off, flickering every time a cloud passes. With the hysteresis buffer, on below 290, off above 320, the lamp turns on at 280 and stays on at 305, because 305 has not crossed the 320 shutoff point. You can test this exact scenario in study guide chapter 9 and test bank question MC06.",
